A rainbow is a natural optical phenomenon caused by the interaction of sunlight with water droplets in the atmosphere. It is not a physical object but rather an effect of light scattering, reflection, and refraction. Here is the step-by-step process of how it forms:

### 1. Sunlight Enters the Water Droplet
Sunlight appears white, but it is actually composed of all the colors of the visible spectrum (red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet). When this light hits a raindrop, it enters the curved surface of the drop. Because water is denser than air, the light **refracts** (bends) as it passes from the air into the water. Different colors bend at slightly different angles because they have different wavelengths;
